  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of rain and breezy conditions from \nIsaias arrive tonight and continue through Sunday \n\nHigh to mid level clouds will continues to expand over CPA this\nevening into tonight. Rain associated with Isaias is projected \nto reach the Laurel Highlands/southern Alleghenies around midnight\nand gradually expand to the north/east across central PA into \nSunday morning. Periods of rain continue Sunday morning/afternoon\ninto Sunday night before tapering off by early Monday. There has\nbeen an uptick in QPF along with the probability of 2\" of rain\nover parts of south central into southeast PA/Lower Susq Valley.\nRain may be heavy at times, but aside from any isolated urban\nrunoff issues, we believe there will not be any excessive\nrainfall issues across these areas. The recent dry spell will\nalso provide a cushion. Total rainfall amounts are now \nprojected between 0.50-2.00 over a 36-hr duration between 8PM\ntonight and 8AM Monday. \n\nPost-tropical Isaias tracks from the TN Valley into WV early \nSunday before curving east and exiting the Delmarva/NJ coast \nMonday morning. Expect gusty winds in the tightening pressure \ngradient between the post-tropical low and departing high in \nthe Gulf of Maine. Max gusts in the 45-50 mph range are expected\nin the Laurel Highlands particularly along and to the westside\nof the highest ridgetops (downslope wind from the southeast). \nA Wind Advisory remains in effect for Cambria and Somerset \ncounties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warmer and more humid next week with a couple \nrounds of rain showers possible Tuesday and again late in the \nweek\n\nWarmer and noticeably more humid to start next week. Max temps\non Tuesday are forecast to peak 10-15 degrees above the\nhistorical average for mid October with highs in the 70-80F\nrange. Improving weather conditions can be expected Monday as \nthe remnants of Isaias exit the Mid Atlantic. Rain chances on \nTuesday appear to be maximized over the northern tier where a \nshortwave aloft and sfc cold front (infused with moisture from \nTS Rachel?) streak eastward from the Great Lakes. Another period\nof rain is possible later next week, but there is low confidence\nin the timing of the front.",
